    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Cutaneous melanoma metastasis, liver",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Metastasis (Met) in lower third of field, with replacement histopathological growth pattern. The interface between the metastasis and liver parenchyma (Liv) above is poorly defined. Melanoma cells are dispersed as single cells and in small clusters (arrows) throughout the liver parenchyma considerable distances from the metastasis. These cells replace hepatocytes and are arranged along the external surfaces of sinusoidal microvascular channels in the hepatic plates without altering their architecture. <strong>Inset:</strong>&nbsp;HMB45 immunostain with red chromogen highlights melanoma cells in the liver parenchyma along the abluminal endothelial surfaces of sinusoidal vessels.",
    "source": "Raymond L. Barnhill",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/407/38315.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/64/407/thumb_38315.jpg"
  },
  {
